2. The nature and posture of statistics in state


administration


The key question that a statistics institution faces is


whether it can claim to be independent and what are


the features of the independence. The UN Fundamen-


tal Principles of Ofﬁcial Statistics provide agencies the


base principles for independence and autonomy and the


UN handbook on organisation of statistics ofﬁces give


us instruments for form.
 
Spinner
1
 
goes further and


assists us in understanding the content and context of


what such independence could and should be with fun-


damental implications for form and relationships. Ac-


cording to Spinner in his philosophical treatise, opines


that there are at least three forms of knowledge, rele-


vant to statistics, around which society organises itself.


Statistics form part of this knowledge base.
 
Knowl-


edge can be regarded as information. In that instance,


there is no need for empirical validity.
 
This form of


knowledge is given expression in drama, comedy and


novels.
 
The other form of knowledge is one which


is described as understanding.
 
The attributes of this


form of knowledge are etched in its ability to advance


evidence emanating from scientiﬁc observation.
 
The


third form of knowledge is that which is deﬁned as in-


sight. This form of knowledge draws from understand-


ing and deliberately makes choices, hence the term ev-


idence based decision making.
 
The nature of statis-


tics in state administration is that of providing evidence


as the basis for decision making.
 
The application of


knowledge as understanding and its posture is that of


serving as evidence that informs policy choices in the


ﬁrst instance, but also provides robust systems for ac-


countability. Statistics in state administration becomes


an important arm of advancing transparency and ac-


countability. Given the fact that statistics is the source


