Navigating the implementation of such geo-legal and


constitutional issues for statistics is something that ex-


perts of organisational design and methods will ponder


in years to come.


In the years ahead, where democracy and democratic


practice appear to be the dominant mode of governance,


the question of independence of statistics, its scientiﬁc


base and its role domestically and internationally are


likely to dominate national and global discourse. The


implications of this will be that there is a likelihood of


reforms in the positioning of statistics in government


and state administrations. In Africa, in the majority of


countries especially, the act of addressing statistics as


an institution will be dual.
 
Firstly it will be answer-


ing the question of creating a statistics institution with


some level of visibility as is the case in countries like


Zambia, Botswana, Zimbabwe, Mali and Senegal, but


secondly to legally or constitutionally ponder where it


locates in government and or state machinery as has


been the case in Uganda in relation to the Board of


Directors versus Statistics Council in South Africa and


other countries in the world.
 
It is against this back-


ground that we would explore the development and


role of statistical infrastructure in post-colonial Africa


and what prospects these institutions hold for the future


as statistical reforms receive attention. In fact one of


the biggest impediments to participation by Africans in


statistical fora is the general lack of understanding that


collaboration amongst statistical agencies may be the


biggest international enterprise between and amongst


states.
 
In statistical laws, this is also a mandatory


clause which often is forgotten when this collaboration


requires to be implemented.
 
In fact agencies do not


have an international statistical development ofﬁce in


their structure, a must for any statistical ofﬁce worth or


aspiring to be worth its salt.


